Under L_Fixed: for iso-budget, fixed selection = the K most recent units (R_Block-b: K_units=K; R_Token: K_tok = K×b most recent tokens). So L_Fixed × R_Token attends the most recent K×b tokens — a recency-window of identical size to the blocks case. Good, budget preserved.

Under L_Random: sample K_units units uniformly (blocks) or K_tok tokens uniformly; keep the same expected budget.

L_None: sliding window of fixed size W (e.g., W = the local window used inside InfLLM, say 1024 tokens) — no memory; it's a lower bound. L_Full: all tokens.

Good.

**Key-number of units/tokens:** In the paper, top-K units per head is a hyperparameter. I'll propose K_units ∈ {4, 8} maybe, but to keep design clean, fix K_units = 8 for R_Block-32 (→ 256 tokens attended per head memory) as the reference, and derive others iso-budget. Also the local window (e.g., 1000-2000 tokens) is always attended in InfLLM; keep constant.

